-- Schema version 5 -> 6 (M3: a row the server refuses must not jam the outbox).
--
-- Six additive ALTERs, no back-fill, nothing an existing install can lose. Every new column
-- has a DEFAULT, so rows written before this migration read as "not blocked", which is what
-- they are.
--
-- WHY THIS EXISTS. Validation on the server runs before anything is applied and the whole
-- request is rejected together. Until M3 the client sent its entire outbox in one request,
-- so one row the server would not accept stopped every other row from ever reaching it —
-- including rows that had nothing to do with it. Chunking the push shrinks the blast radius
-- from "everything" to "one page of 200". These columns shrink it the rest of the way: the
-- validation error names the row, so that row is set aside here, with the server's own
-- reason, and the page goes again without it.
--
-- `pending_sync` deliberately STAYS 1 on a blocked row. The row really does still hold an
-- unsent change; it is only excluded from the drain while it is blocked. Clearing the flag
-- would be the same as deciding the change had been sent, which is exactly the lie this
-- milestone is trying not to tell anywhere.
ALTER TABLE person ADD COLUMN sync_blocked INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
ALTER TABLE person ADD COLUMN sync_block_reason TEXT;

ALTER TABLE event ADD COLUMN sync_blocked INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
ALTER TABLE event ADD COLUMN sync_block_reason TEXT;

ALTER TABLE person_trait ADD COLUMN sync_blocked INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
ALTER TABLE person_trait ADD COLUMN sync_block_reason TEXT;
